The user is hitting a real regression, and the line numbers in their stack trace match current `main` exactly (`mcp.ts:587` is the `mkdir`, `mcp.ts:382` is `_ensureSpecs()`), so this is reproducible from our code, not a corrupted install.

**What's happening:** their project directory isn't writable by the container's `nao` user, and since 0.3.0 the MCP layer needs to write into it on every single chat request.

The 0.3.0 MCP rework ([Feat/new mcp #1049](https://github.com/getnao/nao/pull/1049), the first commit after `v0.2.12`) changed MCP tools from being loaded into the context window to being materialized on disk as per-tool OpenAPI specs under `<project>/agent/mcps/<server>/<tool>.json`. That made the project folder a write target on the hot path. Every chat request goes through this before the agent even exists:

```62:63:apps/backend/src/handlers/agent.ts
	await mcpService.initializeMcpState(projectId);
	await skillService.initializeSkills(projectId);
```

`initializeMcpState` → `_initialize` → `_ensureSpecs` → `_discoverServer` → `_writeEnabledSpecs`, which does an unguarded `mkdir`:

```586:588:apps/backend/src/services/mcp.ts
		const dir = this._serverDir(name);
		await mkdir(dir, { recursive: true });
		await this._clearSpecFiles(dir);
```

Every other filesystem and network operation in that file is defensive — per-server discovery failures are caught and recorded in `_failedConnections`, `_clearSpecFiles` swallows errors, `_ensureGitignore` is explicitly best-effort. This `mkdir` is the one that isn't, so an `EACCES` propagates all the way out of `initializeMcpState` and kills the request at handler line 62, before the model, tools, or DB connection are ever touched. That's exactly why the user sees a "MCP error" on a question that has nothing to do with MCP.

It's also permanently stuck rather than intermittent: `initializeMcpState` sets `_initPromise = null` in its catch, so each new message retries the same failing `mkdir` and fails identically.

**Why their project dir isn't writable:** the container runs the backend as the non-root `nao` user (`user=nao` in `docker/supervisord.conf`), but the image only chowns `/app`, `/app/context`, and `/app/projects` — never `/app/project`, which is user-supplied. So this breaks if they bind-mount a host directory owned by a different UID, mount it read-only, or follow our own self-hosting docs, which tell people to build a derived image with `COPY . /app/project/` (in `nao-agent/self-hosting/deployment-guide.mdx` and `nao-agent/context-builder/git-repository.mdx`) — that copies as root with no `--chown=nao:nao`. Before 0.3.0 nothing wrote into the project directory during a chat, so a root-owned or read-only project worked fine and the upgrade silently made writability a hard requirement.

One nasty detail worth knowing before you reply to them: the `mkdir` happens *before* the enablement check.

```586:592:apps/backend/src/services/mcp.ts
		const dir = this._serverDir(name);
		await mkdir(dir, { recursive: true });
		await this._clearSpecFiles(dir);

		if (disabled.servers.has(name)) {
			return;
		}
```

So the obvious workaround — disabling the airtable connector in the admin UI — won't unblock them. They'd have to delete the server from `agent/mcps/mcp.json` entirely, or fix the permissions (`chown -R nao:nao` on the project dir, or `--chown=nao:nao` in their `COPY`).

The durable fixes are to make spec writing non-fatal (catch per-server, surface it as a `_failedConnections` entry the way discovery errors already are) so a broken MCP filesystem degrades to "MCP unavailable" instead of "chat down", and to move the `mkdir` after the `disabled` check. Want me to implement those?

I'm piecing together how the system works. Version 0.3.0 introduced a new MCP architecture where tool specifications are stored as individual JSON files in the project's agent directory rather than loaded into memory. Each chat request triggers an initialization step that sets up the MCP state before the agent processes anything.

The problem is that this initialization chain—discovering servers, writing spec files, creating directories—has an unguarded filesystem operation. When `mkdir` fails due to permission issues, the error bubbles up uncaught and rejects the entire request, even for non-MCP chats. Since the promise isn't cleared on failure, every subsequent message hits the same error. The root cause is likely a permissions mismatch: the backend runs as a non-root user in their container, but the project directory is either a bind mount from the host with different ownership, a read-only mount, or copied during the Docker build as root-owned.

The mkdir happens before the check for disabled servers, so even disabling the connector in the admin UI won't help—users would need to manually remove it from the config file. And this only manifests on fresh upgrades when the server directory doesn't exist yet; if specs were already present, the mkdir never runs. The stack trace line numbers match exactly with the current source code — `mkdir` at line 587 and `_ensureSpecs()` at line 382 — which confirms this is version 0.3.0 and validates the diagnosis. I'm ready to write up the answer.
